C is one of the oldest and most widely used programming languages, developed by Dennis Ritchie at Bell Labs between 1971 and 1973. It is a structured, procedural, and general-purpose programming language that serves as the foundation for many modern programming languages and systems.C is widely used for developing operating systems, compilers, embedded systems, databases, network drivers, and system software. Interview questions on C help evaluate your understanding of programming fundamentals, memory management, pointers, data structures, and problem-solving skills. This article covers the most frequently asked C interview questions, arranged from beginner to intermediate and advanced levels to help you prepare effectively.C Programming Interview Questions - For Freshers1. Why is C called a mid-level programming language?C is called a mid-level programming language because it combines the features of both low-level and high-level programming languages. It provides direct access to hardware while also supporting structured programming for application development.C supports low-level programming through features like pointers, memory manipulation, and inline assembly (asm/__asm__).It also provides high-level programming features such as functions, loops, conditionals, and structured programming, making it suitable for both system and application development.Example:Low-level use: Accessing hardware or memory directly using pointers.High-level use: Writing applications using functions, loops, and conditional statements.2. What are the features of the C programming language? Procedural Programming: Organizes programs into functions, making code modular and easier to understand.Modularity: Large programs can be divided into smaller, reusable functions for better maintenance. Statically Typed: All variables must be declared with a data type before they are used, helping detect errors at compile time. Compiled Language: C code is compiled into machine code before execution, resulting in fast program performance. General-Purpose Language: Can be used to develop system software, application software, embedded systems, and compilers. Rich Set of Built-in Operators: Provides arithmetic, logical, relational, bitwise, assignment, and other operators for efficient programming. Rich Standard Library: Includes a wide range of built-in functions for input/output, string handling, memory management, mathematics, and file operations. Middle-Level Language: Combines the features of both high-level and low-level languages, allowing direct memory access while maintaining readable syntax. Portability: Programs written in C can be compiled and executed on different platforms with minimal modifications. Easy to Extend: Developers can create custom functions and libraries, making C flexible and scalable for large applications.3. What are basic data types supported in the C Programming Language?C provides several basic data types that are used to store different kinds of values, such as integers, characters, and floating-point numbers. Each data type has a specific size and range depending on the system architecture.The five fundamental data types in C are int, char, float, double, and void.These data types form the foundation for creating variables, functions, arrays, pointers, and user-defined data types.Data TypeDescriptionintStores integer values.charStores a single character.floatStores single-precision floating-point values.doubleStores double-precision floating-point values.voidRepresents the absence of a value or type.4. What are tokens in C?Tokens are the smallest meaningful units of a C program that the compiler recognizes. Every C program is made up of different types of tokens.Tokens are the basic building blocks of a C program and help the compiler understand the program.The main types of tokens in C are keywords, identifiers, constants, strings, operators, and special symbols.TokenDescriptionKeywordsReserved words with predefined meanings (e.g., int, if, return).IdentifiersUser-defined names for variables, functions, and arrays.ConstantsFixed values that cannot be changed during program execution.StringsA sequence of characters enclosed in double quotes (" ").OperatorsSymbols used to perform operations (e.g., +, -, *, ==).Special SymbolsSymbols with predefined meanings such as (), {}, [], ;, #, and ,.5. What do you mean by the scope of the variable?The scope of a variable defines the region of a program where the variable can be accessed. Once the execution moves outside its scope, the variable is no longer accessible.The scope determines the visibility and lifetime of a variable within a program. Based on where they are declared, variables in C are classified into local variables, global variables, and function parameters (formal parameters).ScopeDescriptionLocal VariableDeclared inside a function or block and accessible only within that block.Global VariableDeclared outside all functions and accessible throughout the program.Formal ParameterVariables declared in a function's parameter list that receive values when the function is called.6. What are preprocessor directives in C?Preprocessor directives are special instructions that are processed by the C preprocessor before the actual compilation of the program. They begin with the # symbol and are used to control various aspects of the compilation process.Preprocessor directives are commonly used for macro definitions, header file inclusion, and conditional compilation.Some commonly used directives are #define, #include, #if, #ifdef, #undef, and #pragma.DirectivePurpose#includeIncludes a header file.#defineDefines a macro or constant.#if, #ifdefPerforms conditional compilation.#undefRemoves a previously defined macro.#pragmaProvides compiler-specific instructions.Processor in C7. What is the use of static variables in C?A static variable retains its value between multiple function calls and is initialized only once during program execution. Unlike local variables, it is not destroyed when the function exits.A static variable preserves its value across function calls, making it useful for maintaining state. If not explicitly initialized, a static variable is automatically initialized to 0.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

void countCalls() {
    static int count = 0;
    count++;
    printf("%d\n", count);
}

int main() {
    countCalls();
    countCalls();
    countCalls();
    return 0;
}

Output1
2
3
ExplanationThe static variable count is initialized only once and retains its value after each function call. As a result, its value increments on every call instead of being reset to 0.8. What is the difference between malloc() and calloc() in the C programming language?malloc()calloc()Allocates a single block of memory.Allocates multiple contiguous blocks of memory.Takes one argument (total memory size in bytes).Takes two arguments (number of elements and size of each element).Allocated memory is not initialized (contains garbage values).Allocated memory is initialized to zero.Faster because it does not initialize memory.Slightly slower due to memory initialization.Syntax: malloc(size);Syntax: calloc(n, size);9. What do you mean by dangling pointers and how are dangling pointers different from memory leaks in C programming?A dangling pointer is a pointer that continues to point to a memory location after the memory has been deallocated. A memory leak occurs when dynamically allocated memory is not released using free(), causing memory to remain occupied unnecessarily.A dangling pointer refers to invalid (freed) memory, whereas a memory leak occurs when allocated memory becomes unreachable without being freed.Dangling pointers can lead to undefined behavior, while memory leaks gradually increase memory usage and can degrade application performance.Dangling PointerMemory LeakPoints to memory that has already been freed.Allocated memory is never freed.Occurs after deallocating memory while the pointer still exists.Occurs when allocated memory becomes unreachable without calling free().Can cause undefined behavior or crashes when dereferenced.Increases memory usage and may slow down or crash long-running programs.Fixed by assigning the pointer to NULL after free().Fixed by ensuring every allocated memory block is properly released using free().10. Write a program to convert a number to a string with the help of sprintf() function in the C library.sprintf() is a standard library function that converts formatted data, such as numbers, into a string and stores it in a character array.It writes the formatted output to a string instead of displaying it on the screen.It is commonly used to convert numbers into strings using format specifiers like %d, %f, and %c.
            C

    // C program to convert number to string using sprintf()
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

// Driver code
int main()
{
    char res[20];
    float a = 32.23;
    sprintf(res, "%f", a);
    printf("\nThe string for the num is %s", res);
    return 0;
}

OutputThe string for the num is 32.23000011. What is recursion in C?Recursion is a programming technique in which a function calls itself directly or indirectly to solve a problem. It breaks a large problem into smaller subproblems until a base case is reached.Every recursive function must have a base case to stop further recursive calls and prevent infinite recursion. Recursive calls are managed using the call stack (LIFO), so each call consumes additional stack memory.Example
            C

    int factorial(int n) {
    if (n == 0)
        return 1;
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

ExplanationThe factorial() function calls itself with a smaller value of n until it reaches the base case (n == 0). After reaching the base case, the recursive calls return one by one to produce the final result.12. What is the difference between the local and global variables in C?Local variables are declared inside a block or function but global variables are declared outside the block or function to be accessed globally.Local VariablesGlobal VariablesDeclared inside a block or a function.Variables that are declared outside the block or a function.By default, variables store a garbage value.By default value of the global value is zero.The life of the local variables is destroyed after the block or a function.The life of the global variable exists until the program is executed.Variables are stored inside the stack unless they are specified by the programmer.The storage location of the global variable is decided by the compiler.To access the local variables in other functions parameter passing is required.No parameter passing is required. They are globally visible throughout the program.13. What are pointers and their uses? A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable. Pointers are widely used in C for efficient memory management and direct access to data.Pointers improve program efficiency by allowing direct memory access and avoiding unnecessary data copying.They are essential for dynamic memory allocation, implementing data structures, and passing arguments by reference.Common Uses of PointersPassing arguments to functions by reference. Accessing and traversing array elements efficiently. Returning multiple values from a function. Dynamically allocating and freeing memory using malloc() and free(). Implementing data structures such as linked lists, stacks, queues, and trees. Performing system-level programming and direct memory manipulation.
            C

    int x = 10;
int *ptr = &x;

Here, ptr stores the address of x and can be used to access or modify its value.Working of Pointer14. What is typedef in C?The typedef keyword in C is used to create a new name (alias) for an existing data type. It improves code readability and makes complex type declarations easier to use.typedef does not create a new data type; it simply provides an alternate name for an existing one.It is commonly used with structures, pointers, and long data types to make code simpler and more readable.Syntaxtypedef existing_type alias_name;
            C

    typedef long long ll;

ll num = 1000000000;

15. What are loops and how can we create an infinite loop in C?A loop is a control statement used to execute a block of code repeatedly until a specified condition is met. C supports both entry-controlled (for, while) and exit-controlled (do...while) loops.An infinite loop is a loop that continues to execute indefinitely because its termination condition is never met.Infinite loops are commonly created intentionally using while(1) or for(;;), or unintentionally due to an incorrect loop condition.Types of Loops
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

// Driver code
int main()
{
    for (;;) {
        printf("Infinite-loop\n");
    }

    while (1) {
        printf("Infinite-loop\n");
    }

    do {
        printf("Infinite-loop\n");
    } while (1);

    return 0;
}

16. What is the difference between type casting and type conversion?Type CastingType ConversionThe data type is converted to another data type by a programmer with the help of a casting operator.The data type is converted to another data by a compiler.It can be applied to both compatible data types as well as incompatible data types.Type conversion can only be applied to only compatible data types.In Type casting in order to cast the data type into another data type, a caste operator is neededIn type conversion, there is no need for a casting operator.Type casting is more efficient and reliable.Type conversion is less efficient and less reliable than type casting.Type casting takes place during the program design by the programmer.Type conversion is done at compile time.Syntax: destination_data_type = (target_data_type) variable_to_be_converted;Syntax: int a = 20; float b; b = a; // a = 20.0000 17. What are header files and their uses?A header file is a file that contains function declarations, macros, constants, and data type definitions that can be reused across multiple C programs. It is included using the #include preprocessor directive.Header files are of two types: standard (predefined) and user-defined header files.They promote code reusability, reduce duplication, and provide access to predefined library functions.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!");
    return 0;
}

Here, the stdio.h header file provides the declaration of the printf() function used for output.18. What are the functions and their types?A function is a block of code that performs a specific task and can be called whenever needed. Functions help reduce code duplication, improve readability, and make programs easier to maintain.C provides two types of functions: user-defined functions and library (built-in) functions.Functions make programs modular, reusable, and easier to debug.Function TypeDescriptionUser-defined FunctionsFunctions created by the programmer to perform specific tasks.Library (Built-in) FunctionsPredefined functions provided by C libraries, such as printf(), scanf(), and strlen().
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

void greet() {
    printf("Welcome!\n");
}

int main() {
    greet();
    return 0;
}

Here, greet() is a user-defined function, while printf() is a library function.19. What is the difference between macro and functions?A macro is a name that is given to a block of C statements as a pre-processor directive. Macro is defined with the pre-processor directive. Macros are pre-processed which means that all the macros would be preprocessed before the compilation of our program. However, functions are not preprocessed but compiled. MacroFunctionMacros are preprocessed.Functions are compiled.Code length is increased using macro.Code length remains unaffected using function.Execution speed using a macro is faster.Execution speed using function is slower.The macro name is replaced by the macro value before compilation.Transfer of control takes place during the function call.Macro doesn't check any Compile-Time Errors.Function check Compile-time errors.20. What is the difference between declaration and definition in C?A declaration tells the compiler about the name and type of a variable or function, whereas a definition allocates memory for it or provides its actual implementation.A variable or function can be declared multiple times, but it can be defined only once in a program.Declarations help the compiler recognize an identifier before it is used, while definitions create the actual object or function.DeclarationDefinitionIntroduces the variable or function to the compiler.Creates the variable or function.Does not allocate memory (for variables using extern).Allocates memory for variables or provides the function body.Can appear multiple times.Can appear only once.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

extern int num;   // Declaration

int num = 10;     // Definition

int main() {
    printf("%d", num);
    return 0;
}

Output1021. What is the Difference Between Compile-Time Errors, Runtime Errors, and Logical Errors?Errors in C programs can occur during compilation, execution, or due to incorrect program logic. Understanding these errors helps in debugging programs effectively.Compile-time errors are detected by the compiler before the program runs. Runtime errors occur while the program is executing, whereas logical errors produce incorrect output without generating any error message.Error TypeDescriptionExampleCompile-Time ErrorDetected by the compiler before execution due to syntax or semantic mistakes.Missing ;, undeclared variableRuntime ErrorOccurs while the program is running due to invalid operations.Division by zero, invalid memory accessLogical ErrorThe program runs successfully but produces incorrect results due to faulty logic.Using + instead of * in a formula
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 0;

    // Runtime error
    printf("%d", a / b);

    return 0;
}

ExplanationCompile-time errors prevent the program from compiling. Runtime errors occur during execution and may cause the program to terminate. Logical errors are the hardest to detect because the program runs normally but produces incorrect output.22. What is the Difference Between printf() and scanf()?printf() and scanf() are standard input/output functions provided by the <stdio.h> library. printf() is used to display output, while scanf() is used to read input from the user.printf() sends formatted data to the output screen, whereas scanf() reads formatted data from the keyboard. scanf() requires the address of variables (using & for most data types), while printf() prints the variable values directly.printf()scanf()Used to display output on the screen.Used to read input from the user.Writes data to the standard output (stdout).Reads data from the standard input (stdin).Prints the value of variables.Stores user input into variables.Does not require & before variables.Requires & before variables (except character arrays/strings).23. What is the Difference Between = and == in C?The = operator is an assignment operator used to assign a value to a variable, whereas == is a relational operator used to compare two values.= assigns the value on the right-hand side to the variable on the left-hand side.== checks whether two values are equal and returns true (1) or false (0).===Assignment operator.Equality (comparison) operator.Assigns a value to a variable.Compares two values for equality.Does not return a Boolean result.Returns 1 (true) or 0 (false).Example: a = 10;Example: a == 10
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a = 10;   // Assignment

    if (a == 10)  // Comparison
        printf("Equal");

    return 0;
}

OutputEqualExplanationa = 10 assigns the value 10 to the variable a.a == 10 checks whether a is equal to 10. Since the condition is true, "Equal" is printed.24. What is the Purpose of the sizeof Operator in C?The sizeof operator is used to determine the size (in bytes) of a data type, variable, or expression at compile time. It helps in memory allocation and writing portable programs.sizeof returns the memory occupied by a variable or data type in bytes. It is commonly used with arrays, structures, and dynamic memory allocation (malloc()).
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int num = 10;

    printf("Size of int: %zu bytes\n", sizeof(int));
    printf("Size of num: %zu bytes", sizeof(num));

    return 0;
}

OutputSize of int: 4 bytes
Size of num: 4 bytes25. What is a Character Array? How is It Different from a String?A character array is an array that stores individual characters. A string is a character array that ends with a special null character ('\0'), which marks the end of the string.Every string is a character array, but not every character array is a string. A character array becomes a string only when it is null-terminated ('\0').Character ArrayStringStores a sequence of characters.Stores a sequence of characters ending with '\0'.May or may not be null-terminated.Always null-terminated.Cannot always be used with string library functions.Can be used with string functions like strlen() and strcpy().
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    char arr[] = {'H', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o'};
    char str[] = "Hello";

    printf("%s", str);

    return 0;
}

OutputHelloExplanationarr is a character array without a null terminator, so it is not a string. str is a string because the compiler automatically appends the null character ('\0').26. What is the Difference Between while and do-while Loops?Both while and do-while are looping statements used to execute a block of code repeatedly. The main difference is when the loop condition is checked.In a while loop, the condition is checked before executing the loop body. In a do-while loop, the loop body executes at least once because the condition is checked after execution.while Loopdo-while LoopCondition is checked before execution.Condition is checked after execution.May execute zero or more times.Executes at least one time.Entry-controlled loop.Exit-controlled loop.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 1;

    while (i <= 3) {
        printf("%d ", i);
        i++;
    }

    return 0;
}

Output1 2 3 ExplanationThe while loop checks the condition before each iteration. In a do-while loop, the loop body executes first and the condition is checked afterward, ensuring at least one execution.27. What are Escape Sequences in C? Give Some Examples.Escape sequences are special character combinations that begin with a backslash (\). They are used to represent special characters that cannot be typed directly in a string or character constant.Escape sequences are commonly used for formatting output, such as creating a new line or inserting a tab. Each escape sequence starts with \ followed by a specific character.Escape SequenceDescription\nNew line\tHorizontal tab\\Backslash (\)\"Double quotation mark\'Single quotation mark\0Null character\bBackspace\rCarriage return
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello\nWorld");
    return 0;
}

OutputHello
WorldExplanation\n inserts a new line, causing World to be printed on the next line. Escape sequences make it easier to format text and represent special characters in C programs.28. What is the Difference Between Local and Global Variables?A local variable is declared inside a function or block and can only be accessed within that scope. A global variable is declared outside all functions and can be accessed throughout the program.Local variables exist only while the function or block is executing, whereas global variables exist for the entire program execution. Local variables are accessible only within their scope, while global variables can be accessed by all functions.Local VariableGlobal VariableDeclared inside a function or block.Declared outside all functions.Accessible only within its scope.Accessible throughout the program.Created when the function is called and destroyed when it exits.Exists for the entire program execution.Not automatically initialized.Automatically initialized to 0 if not explicitly initialized.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int globalVar = 100;

void display() {
    int localVar = 10;

    printf("Local Variable: %d\n", localVar);
    printf("Global Variable: %d\n", globalVar);
}

int main() {
    display();
    return 0;
}

OutputLocal Variable: 10
Global Variable: 100
ExplanationlocalVar is a local variable and can only be accessed inside the display() function. globalVar is a global variable and can be accessed from any function in the program.29. How are Arrays Initialized in C?An array in C can be initialized at the time of declaration by providing values enclosed in curly braces {}. If fewer values are provided than the array size, the remaining elements are automatically initialized to 0.Arrays can be initialized with all values, partial values, or by letting the compiler determine the array size. Multidimensional arrays are also initialized using nested curly braces.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int arr1[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int arr2[5] = {1, 2};
    int arr3[] = {5, 10, 15};

    printf("%d %d %d\n", arr1[0], arr2[2], arr3[1]);

    return 0;
}

Output10 0 10
Explanationarr1 is fully initialized with five elements. arr2 is partially initialized, so the remaining elements are automatically set to 0. arr3 lets the compiler determine the array size based on the number of values provided.30. What is the Difference Between ++i and i++ in C?Both ++i and i++ are increment operators used to increase the value of a variable by 1. The difference lies in when the increment takes place.++i (pre-increment) increments the variable first and then returns the updated value. i++ (post-increment) returns the current value first and then increments the variable.++i (Pre-increment)i++ (Post-increment)Increments the value before it is used.Uses the current value before incrementing it.Returns the updated value.Returns the original value, then increments it.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 5;

    printf("%d\n", ++i);
    i = 5;
    printf("%d", i++);

    return 0;
}

Output6
5Explanation++i increments i to 6 before printing it.i++ prints the current value (5) first and then increments i to 6.C Programming Interview Questions - Intermediate Level31. What is the Difference Between malloc(), calloc(), realloc(), and free()?These are dynamic memory management functions provided by the <stdlib.h> library. They are used to allocate, resize, and release memory during program execution.malloc(), calloc(), and realloc() allocate or modify heap memory, while free() releases it. Memory allocated using these functions must be released using free() to avoid memory leaks.FunctionPurposemalloc()Allocates a single block of memory without initialization.calloc()Allocates multiple memory blocks and initializes them to 0.realloc()Resizes an existing memory block while preserving its data.free()Releases dynamically allocated memory back to the system.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int *arr = (int *)malloc(5 * sizeof(int));

    arr = (int *)realloc(arr, 10 * sizeof(int));

    free(arr);

    return 0;
}

Explanationmalloc() allocates memory for 5 integers. realloc() resizes the memory block to store 10 integers. free() releases the allocated memory, preventing memory leaks.32. What is Pointer Arithmetic? Explain with an Example.Pointer arithmetic refers to performing arithmetic operations on pointers to move between memory locations. It is mainly used to traverse arrays and access elements efficiently.You can increment (++), decrement (--), add, or subtract an integer from a pointer. When a pointer is incremented, it moves to the next element based on the size of its data type.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30};
    int *ptr = arr;

    printf("%d\n", *ptr);

    ptr++;

    printf("%d", *ptr);

    return 0;
}

Output10
20ExplanationInitially, ptr points to the first element (10) of the array. After ptr++, it moves to the next integer in memory and points to 20.33. What is a Double Pointer in C?A double pointer (pointer to a pointer) is a pointer that stores the address of another pointer. It is declared using two asterisks (**).A double pointer is commonly used to modify a pointer inside a function and to work with dynamic memory allocation. It provides an additional level of indirection, allowing access to the original variable through another pointer.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int num = 10;
    int *ptr = &num;
    int **dptr = &ptr;

    printf("Value = %d", **dptr);

    return 0;
}

OutputValue = 10Explanationptr stores the address of num. dptr stores the address of ptr, and **dptr accesses the value of num (10).34. What is a Void Pointer?A void pointer (void *) is a generic pointer that can store the address of any data type. Since it has no associated data type, it must be typecast before dereferencing.A void pointer can point to variables of any data type, making it useful for generic programming. It cannot be dereferenced directly because the compiler does not know the type of data it points to.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int num = 10;
    void *ptr = &num;

    printf("%d", *(int *)ptr);

    return 0;
}

Output10Explanationptr is a void pointer that stores the address of num. Before accessing the value, ptr is typecast to int * because a void pointer cannot be dereferenced directly.35. What are NULL, Wild, Dangling, and Void Pointers?These are special types of pointers in C that differ based on how they are initialized and what they point to.Each pointer type has a specific purpose, and improper use can lead to undefined behavior. Understanding these pointers helps prevent common memory-related bugs.Pointer TypeDescriptionNULL PointerA pointer initialized to NULL that does not point to any valid memory location.Wild PointerA pointer that has been declared but not initialized, so it contains a garbage address.Dangling PointerA pointer that points to memory that has already been deallocated or is no longer valid.Void PointerA generic pointer (void *) that can store the address of any data type but must be typecast before dereferencing.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int *nullPtr = NULL;       // NULL pointer

    int *wildPtr;              // Wild pointer

    int *ptr = (int *)malloc(sizeof(int));
    free(ptr);                 // ptr becomes a dangling pointer

    int num = 10;
    void *voidPtr = &num;      // Void pointer

    return 0;
}

ExplanationnullPtr points to no valid memory location. wildPtr is uninitialized and contains a garbage address. ptr becomes a dangling pointer after the allocated memory is freed. voidPtr stores the address of num and must be typecast before dereferencing.36. What is the Difference Between an Array and a Pointer?An array is a collection of elements of the same data type stored in contiguous memory locations, whereas a pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable.An array has a fixed size, while a pointer can point to different memory locations. The name of an array represents the address of its first element, but it is not a pointer.ArrayPointerStores a collection of elements.Stores the address of a variable or memory location.Size is fixed after declaration.Can point to different memory locations.Memory is allocated automatically.Can point to dynamically allocated memory.Array name cannot be reassigned.A pointer variable can be reassigned.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30};
    int *ptr = arr;

    printf("%d\n", arr[0]);
    printf("%d", *ptr);

    return 0;
}

Output10
10Explanationarr is an array that stores three integers. ptr points to the first element of the array, so *ptr and arr[0] access the same value.37. What is the Difference Between strcpy() and strncpy()?Both strcpy() and strncpy() are string handling functions declared in the <string.h> library and are used to copy one string to another. The main difference is how many characters they copy.strcpy() copies the entire source string, including the null character ('\0'). strncpy() copies only the specified number of characters and may not append a null character if the source string is longer than the specified limit.strcpy()strncpy()Copies the entire source string.Copies up to a specified number of characters.Always appends the null character ('\0').May not append '\0' if the limit is reached.Can cause buffer overflow if the destination is too small.Helps limit copying and reduce buffer overflow risk.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char src[] = "GeeksforGeeks";
    char dest[20];

    strncpy(dest, src, 5);
    dest[5] = '\0';

    printf("%s", dest);

    return 0;
}

OutputGeeksExplanationstrncpy(dest, src, 5) copies only the first 5 characters from src. Since strncpy() may not automatically add the null character, dest[5] = '\0'; is used to terminate the string properly.38. What is the Difference Between strcmp() and strncmp()?Both strcmp() and strncmp() are string comparison functions declared in the <string.h> library. They compare two strings lexicographically, but they differ in how many characters they compare.strcmp() compares the entire strings until a difference is found or the null character ('\0') is reached. strncmp() compares only the first n characters of the two strings.strcmp()strncmp()Compares the entire strings.Compares only the first n characters.Stops at the first mismatch or '\0'.Stops after comparing n characters or at '\0'.Takes two arguments.Takes three arguments (two strings and n).
            C

    #include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char str1[] = "GeeksforGeeks";
    char str2[] = "GeeksQuiz";

    if (strncmp(str1, str2, 5) == 0)
        printf("First 5 characters are equal");
    else
        printf("First 5 characters are different");

